Thanks for your help Joaquin and Hans! Just in case others need to do this, here is what I ended up with following the advice in this thread.

The function dsn() is standardized to N(0,1) and calculated as <https://kaskr.github.io/adcomp/distributions__R_8hpp_source.html>: 2 * dnorm(x,Type(0),Type(1),0) * pnorm(alpha*x);
The density of the general skew normal is given by this equation <https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/f6b462dbbe3ef89df7334f0e198056637ed8e2fc>, where delta, omega, and xi are parameters that can be solved for given some mean and variance according to the equations on the right side of this wikipedia article <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Skew_normal_distribution>. So, to adapt the TMB function dsn() to give the density of x given mu, sigma, and alpha, we basically need to substitute (x - xi)/omega in for x and divide by omega so that the TMB function matches the definition of the skew normal density. In TMB, this looks like:

Given mean mu, standard deviation sigma, and slant parameter alpha:

// convert mu, sigma, alpha to parameters needed for skew normal

  delta = alpha/sqrt(1+pow(alpha,2));

  omega = sqrt(pow(sigma,2)/(1-(2*pow(delta,2))/pi));

  xi = mu - omega*delta*sqrt(2/pi);

// calculate density over values of x

for (int i = 0; i < nx; i++){
d(i) = dsn((x(i) - xi)/omega, alpha, false)/omega;
}

